# Jaguar S-Type 3.8, 1963-1968.
#
# The S-Type was the Mk 2 body with Jaguar's new independent rear suspension and a
# restyled tail, sold alongside the Mk 2 from 1963 to 1968. It is the compact
# luxury saloon of the mid-1960s: twin round headlamps with smaller inboard lamps,
# a tall oval grille of vertical chrome bars, chrome bumpers with overriders and a
# chrome waist strip.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jaguar_S-Type_(1963)
#
# Dimensions 4.750 x 1.683 x 1.416 m, wheelbase 2.730 m; the published S-Type
# dimension box.
#
# Colours: British Racing Green, Carmen Red, Old English White and Opalescent
# Silver Grey, Jaguar offerings of the period. Pre-1994 registrations have no
# measured colour split, so the weights follow the register's treatment of
# low-volume prestige cars rather than a colour-share calculation: green 3, red 2,
# white 2, silver 1. Total 8. Provisional pending the central rebase.
#
# Speed 190 km/h, near the 3.8's real maximum but held to the pakset's executive
# tier. Comfort 90/315, on the line 180 + 1.5 x 90: a quiet, well-sprung saloon but
# with the short-travel ride and wind noise of its decade. Class 3, the high wealth
# band, as the Jaguar Mk 2 already in the tree.
#
# Dates are road presence, not production: October 1963 intro, retires staggered
# 1978-1985 on a fifteen- to twenty-two-year tail.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/jaguar-s-type-1963.blend. Interchange:
# citycars/4.5/jaguar-s-type-1963.dae (280 objects, the count inflated by the wire
# wheels). References: citycars/references/jaguar-s-type-1963/ (gitignored;
# Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.0).
#
# Accepted low-poly deviations, recorded rather than corrected: the body and cabin
# curvature is more angular than the photographed compound curves, and the wire
# wheels are modelled although one reference photograph shows the disc-hubcap
# alternative.

